      "type": "agentic",
      "position": {
        "x": 267.66768727091653,
        "y": -120.26025868627677
      },
      "parameters": {
        "prompt": "You measure ONE dimension of patch risk: the KNOWN STATE of the patch itself — vendor track record, known issues with these updates, anything about the fix that argues caution. Facts from Identify: affected_count=${identify.artifacts.affected_count}, affected_cves=${identify.artifacts.affected_cves}, max_severity=${identify.artifacts.max_severity}, kev_present=${identify.artifacts.kev_present}, has_vendor_fix=${identify.artifacts.has_vendor_fix}, eol_exposed=${identify.artifacts.eol_exposed}. Target host: ${trigger.target_hostname}. CVEs in scope: ${trigger.cve_ids}. You have NO internet access and no external feeds in this environment: reason only from the vendor/package/fix facts given, state that limitation in reasons, and keep confidence low (<=0.5) when the signal is thin. Never invent vendor incidents. impact = severity of the patch problems you can substantiate; importance = how strong and relevant that signal is here. Return JSON only: {impact, importance, reasons, confidence}. Both scores 0-100. impact = how severe the consequences in YOUR dimension are; importance = how much weight your dimension deserves in THIS decision given the context. You do NOT classify risk — a policy engine derives the risk band from your two scores; report measurements, not conclusions. reasons: specific factual reasons joined by '; '. confidence 0-1 reflecting how much real data you had (thin data means low confidence; never invent facts).",
